public class EventBus {

    // Event type → list of subscribers for that type
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Class<?>,
        CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription<?>>> registry
            = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private final ExecutorService asyncPool
        = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
            Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(),
            r -> {
                Thread t = new Thread(r, "eventbus-worker");
                t.setDaemon(true);  // don't block JVM shutdown
                return t;
            });

    // Type-safe subscribe: returns token for unsubscription
    public <E> Subscription<E> subscribe(Class<E> eventType,
                                          Consumer<E> handler) {
        Subscription<E> sub = new Subscription<>(eventType, handler);
        registry.computeIfAbsent(eventType,
            k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(sub);
        return sub;  // caller keeps this to unsubscribe later
    }

    public void unsubscribe(Subscription<?> sub) {
        var subs = registry.get(sub.getEventType());
        if (subs != null) subs.remove(sub);
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <E> void publish(E event) {
        var subs = registry.get(event.getClass());
        if (subs == null) return;

        for (Subscription<?> sub : subs) {
            Subscription<E> typed = (Subscription<E>) sub;
            asyncPool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    typed.getHandler().accept(event);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // Isolate: one failing subscriber
                    // must not affect others
                    System.err.println("Subscriber failed: " + e);
                }
            });
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        asyncPool.shutdown();
    }
}

// Subscription token — immutable, used for unsubscribe
public class Subscription<E> {
    private final Class<E> eventType;
    private final Consumer<E> handler;

    Subscription(Class<E> eventType, Consumer<E> handler) {
        this.eventType = eventType;
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    Class<E> getEventType() { return eventType; }
    Consumer<E> getHandler() { return handler; }
}

**Why CopyOnWriteArrayList?** Subscriptions change rarely (at startup/shutdown) but are iterated on every publish. COWAL is optimized for exactly this read-heavy pattern — iteration never locks, only mutations copy the internal array. If subscriptions change frequently at runtime, switch to a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` instead.

**Event hierarchy:** the implementation above uses exact-type matching — publishing an `OrderCreatedEvent` does not notify subscribers of its superclass `DomainEvent`. To support hierarchy dispatch, walk the class hierarchy at publish time:

```java
public <E> void publishWithHierarchy(E event) {
    Class<?> type = event.getClass();
    while (type != null) {
        var subs = registry.get(type);
        if (subs != null) {
            for (Subscription<?> sub : subs) {
                asyncPool.submit(() -> {
                    try {
                        ((Consumer) sub.getHandler()).accept(event);
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        System.err.println("Subscriber failed: " + e);
                    }
                });
            }
        }
        type = type.getSuperclass(); // walk up the hierarchy
    }
}
```

Guava EventBus uses this approach with a cache of type hierarchies to avoid repeated reflection. Spring ApplicationEventPublisher resolves the full type hierarchy plus implemented interfaces, which is why a listener on `ApplicationEvent` receives every event.
